returned_idps":null},{"year":2022,"returned_refugees":2000,"resettlement":null,"naturalisation":null,"returned_idps":null},{"year":2023,"returned_refugees":4000,"resettlement":null,"naturalisation":null,"returned_idps":null},{"year":2025,"returned_refugees":null,"resettlement":null,"naturalisation":5,"returned_idps":null}],"asylum_applications":{"host":[{"year":2015,"applied":319},{"year":2016,"applied":342},{"year":2017,"applied":346},{"year":2018,"applied":169},{"year":2019,"applied":184},{"year":2020,"applied":145},{"year":2021,"applied":589},{"year":2022,"applied":541},{"year":2023,"applied":1603},{"year":2024,"applied":1112},{"year":2025,"applied":1698}],"origin":[{"year":2015,"applied":92},{"year":2016,"applied":21},{"year":2017,"applied":20},{"year":2018,"applied":41},{"year":2019,"applied":36},{"year":2020,"applied":10},{"year":2021,"applied":32},{"year":2022,"applied":79},{"year":2023,"applied":149},{"year":2024,"applied":125},{"year":2025,"applied":117}]},"idmc":[],"footnotes":[{"year":null,"population_type":"STA","text":"The Republic of Latvia enacted a Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004, which replaced the Law on the Status of Stateless Persons in the Republic of Latvia of 18 February 1999, and which determines the legal status of persons who are not considered as citizens by the legislation of any State and whose status is not determined by the 25th April 1995 Law (quoted below).","view":"asylum"},{"year":null,"population_type":"STA","text":"This figure includes persons of concern who fall under Latvia’s 25 April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any Other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non-citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2015,"population_type":"STA","text":"With respect to persons under UNHCR’s statelessness mandate, this figure includes persons of concern covered by two separate Latvian laws. 178 persons fall under the Republic of Latvia’s Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004. 252,017 of the persons fall under Latvia’s 25 April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any Other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non-citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2015,"population_type":"STA","text":"The Republic of Latvia enacted a Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004, which replaced the Law on the Status of Stateless Persons in the Republic of Latvia of 18 February 1999, and which determines the legal status of persons who are not considered as citizens by the legislation of any State and whose status is not determined by the 25th April 1995 law (quoted below).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2015,"population_type":"STA","text":"This figure includes persons of concern who fall under Latvia’s 25th April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non- citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2016,"population_type":"STA","text":"The Republic of Latvia enacted a Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004, which replaced the Law on the Status of Stateless Persons in the Republic of Latvia of 18 February 1999, and which determines the legal status of persons who are not considered as citizens by the legislation of any State and whose status is not determined by the 25th April 1995 Law. This figure includes persons of concern who fall under Latvia’s 25 April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any Other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non-citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2016,"population_type":"STA","text":"With respect to persons under UNHCR’s statelessness mandate, this figure includes persons of concern covered by two separate Latvian laws. 176 persons fall under the Republic of Latvia’s Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004. 242,560 of the persons fall under Latvia’s 25 April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any Other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non-citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2017,"population_type":"STA","text":"With respect to persons under UNHCR’s statelessness mandate, this figure includes persons of concern covered by two separate Latvian laws. 178 persons fall under the Republic of Latvia’s Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004. 233,393 of the persons fall under Latvia’s 25 April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any Other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non-citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2018,"population_type":"STA","text":"With respect to persons under UNHCR’s statelessness mandate, this figure includes persons of concern covered by two separate Latvian laws. 174 persons fall under the Republic of Latvia’s Law on Stateless Persons on 17 February 2004. 224,670 of the persons fall under Latvia’s 25 April 1995 Law on the Status of those Former USSR Citizens who are not Citizens of Latvia or Any Other State (“Non-citizens”). In the specific context of Latvia, the “Non-citizens” enjoy the right to reside in Latvia ex lege and a set of rights and obligations generally beyond the rights prescribed by the 1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons, including protection from removal, and as such the “Non-citizens” may currently be considered persons to whom the Convention does not apply in accordance with Article 1.2(ii).","view":"asylum"},{"year":2019,"population_type":"STA","text":"With respect to persons under UNHCR’s statelessness mandate, this figure includes persons of concern covered by two separate Latvian laws.